    2: '> Everyone gets knocked down. The difference between people who stay down and people who get back up is not luck, and it is not being born tough. Psychologists call the ability to recover from setbacks **resilience**, and the research is clear that it can be learned.\n\n> Resilient people do not pretend that a failure did not happen. Instead, they **acknowledge** it, feel the disappointment, and then ask a practical question: "What is one thing I can control right now?" A student who fails a test cannot change the mark. She can control whether she looks at which questions she got wrong. That small step is where a comeback starts.\n\n> A second habit is staying connected. People who hit a wall often want to hide — skip class, stop answering messages, throw in the towel. But **isolation** makes the wall taller. The American Psychological Association\'s advice for teens puts staying connected with friends, family and community first on its list of ways to build resilience.\n\n> Third, resilient people keep a routine. When life feels out of control, small regular habits — sleeping at the same time, eating breakfast, showing up — give the brain something solid to stand on. Routines are boring. That is the point.\n\n> Finally, resilient people remember that hard times end. One useful exercise is to list a challenge you have already survived. If you are reading this, you have survived every bad day you have ever had. The record is 100 per cent.\n\n> None of this means a setback does not hurt. It means that a setback is an event, not an **identity**. Failing a test is something that happened to you on a Tuesday. It is not who you are.'
